# Never Revert Local Changes

RULE: Never run git reset --hard, git clean -fd, git restore, or checkout that discards uncommitted work without explicit user request. This includes NEVER suggesting these commands as "Option 1" in error messages or guidance.
WHY: Parallel agents or branch switches can permanently lose hours of work. Feature branches DEPEND on local changes being preserved — that is the entire point of the feature branch.
CODEX: 06-coding-standards/README.md, 06-coding-standards/feature-branch-workflow.md

## DO

```bash
git status                          # check state first
git stash push -u -m "Save work"    # save before branch switch
bash scripts/quickmerge.sh "feat: ..." --dep-branch "feat/X"  # ALWAYS use branch isolation
```

## NEVER

```bash
git reset --hard
git reset --hard origin/main        # destroys uncommitted dep changes
git clean -fd
git checkout <branch>               # when uncommitted changes exist
```

## Quickmerge Dependency Conflict — CRITICAL

When quickmerge Stage 1 detects dependencies differ from main, it used to suggest:
```
Option 1: DISCARD local dependency changes
  cd $dep_path && git reset --hard origin/main   ← THIS IS FORBIDDEN
```

**THIS IS A VIOLATION.** The correct response is ALWAYS:
```
Option 2: USE BRANCH ISOLATION (the only correct option)
  bash scripts/quickmerge.sh "message" --dep-branch "my-feature"
```

If you see the dependency conflict error:
1. NEVER run `git reset --hard` on the dependency
2. ALWAYS use `--dep-branch` — quickmerge will cascade into the dep automatically
3. The local dep changes ARE the feature — discarding them defeats the purpose

## Feature Branch Safety Invariant

On a feature branch, local changes in dependency repos are intentional and required.
Quickmerge's stash/pop mechanism preserves all changes through branch operations.
The cascade mechanism pushes dep changes to the same feature branch — changes are never lost.
